export default [
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company runs its application on AWS Lambda. Under the shared responsibility model, which task is the customer's responsibility?","o":["Patching the operating system of the execution environment","Scaling the underlying compute fleet","Maintaining the servers that run the functions","Securing the function code and the permissions of its execution role"],"a":[3],"e":"With Lambda, AWS manages the servers, operating system, managed runtimes, and scaling, while the customer owns the function code, its dependencies, and its IAM permissions. Patching the execution environment's OS is AWS's job, not the customer's."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company stores data in Amazon DynamoDB. Which task is the customer's responsibility?","o":["Patching the database software","Replacing failed storage hardware","Defining IAM policies that control who can read and write each table","Replicating data across Availability Zones"],"a":[2],"e":"DynamoDB is fully managed: AWS handles software patching, hardware, and multi-AZ replication. The customer still controls access to its data through IAM policies; patching the database software is not something the customer can or must do."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company stores files in Amazon S3. Which task is the customer's responsibility?","o":["Configuring bucket policies and S3 Block Public Access settings","Ensuring the durability of the underlying storage devices","Securing the facilities that host S3","Patching the S3 storage servers"],"a":[0],"e":"For S3, the customer decides who can access buckets and objects (bucket policies, Block Public Access, encryption choices). Storage device durability, facilities, and server patching are AWS responsibilities."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company moves a self-managed MySQL database from Amazon EC2 to Amazon RDS for MySQL. Which responsibility moves from the customer to AWS?","o":["Managing database user accounts","Choosing which data to store","Controlling network access with security groups","Patching the operating system of the database host"],"a":[3],"e":"With RDS, AWS manages the host OS and database engine patching. The customer still manages database users, the data itself, and security group rules, just as it did on EC2."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Under the shared responsibility model, which responsibilities ALWAYS remain with the customer, no matter which AWS service is used? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Physical security of the data centers","Customer data and how it is classified and protected","Patching the hypervisor","Managing identities and access permissions to its resources","Maintaining the global network infrastructure"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Even for fully managed or serverless services, the customer always owns its data and who can access it (IAM). Physical security, the hypervisor, and the global network are always AWS responsibilities."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which is an example of a customer-specific control in the AWS shared responsibility model?","o":["Physical and environmental controls","Service and communications protection, such as routing data within specific security zones","Patch management","Awareness and training"],"a":[1],"e":"Customer-specific controls are solely the customer's, such as zone security and how data is routed within its environments. Patch management and awareness and training are shared controls, and physical controls are inherited from AWS."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An auditor asks which controls a company fully inherits from AWS when it runs workloads on AWS. Which answer is correct?","o":["Configuration of security groups","Physical and environmental controls of the data centers","Encryption of application data","Guest operating system patching"],"a":[1],"e":"Inherited controls are fully provided by AWS and inherited by the customer, such as physical and environmental controls. Security groups, data encryption, and guest OS patching are configured by the customer."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"How is patch management divided between AWS and the customer for workloads on Amazon EC2?","o":["AWS patches the infrastructure, and the customer patches the guest OS and applications","AWS patches everything, including the guest OS","The customer patches the hypervisor and the guest OS","The customer patches only the physical hosts"],"a":[0],"e":"Patch management is a shared control: AWS patches the infrastructure and hypervisor, while the customer patches the EC2 guest OS and its applications. AWS has no access to patch inside customer instances."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Configuration management is a shared control. What part is the customer responsible for?","o":["Configuring AWS infrastructure devices","Configuring the hardware in AWS data centers","Configuring the hypervisor","Configuring its own guest operating systems, databases, and applications"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS configures its infrastructure devices, while the customer configures its own guest OS, databases, and applications. The hypervisor and hardware belong to AWS."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company uses Amazon RDS. Which task is the customer's responsibility?","o":["Applying minor engine patches to the underlying host","Replacing failed disks in the storage layer","Enabling encryption at rest and managing database user permissions","Maintaining the physical network between Availability Zones"],"a":[2],"e":"With RDS, the customer chooses settings such as encryption at rest, security groups, and database user permissions. AWS handles the host, engine patching, storage hardware, and physical network."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company moves an application from Amazon EC2 to AWS Lambda. How does its share of security responsibility change?","o":["It increases, because the customer must now manage the runtime","It stays exactly the same","It moves completely to AWS, including the code","It decreases, but the customer still secures its code, data, and IAM permissions"],"a":[3],"e":"The more managed the service, the less the customer manages: with Lambda, AWS takes over the OS and runtime. The customer never hands off responsibility for its code, data, and access permissions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Who is responsible for securely destroying AWS storage devices that reach the end of their useful life?","o":["The customer that last stored data on the device","AWS","An AWS Partner chosen by the customer","The AWS Trust & Safety team at the customer's request"],"a":[1],"e":"Media decommissioning is part of AWS's security OF the cloud; AWS sanitizes and destroys devices using its own procedures. Customers never get physical access to AWS hardware."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Under the shared responsibility model, who decides whether traffic between an application's web tier and database tier is encrypted?","o":["The customer","AWS","It is always encrypted by AWS, so no decision is needed","The Availability Zone operator"],"a":[0],"e":"Network traffic protection (encryption, integrity, identity) is part of the customer's security IN the cloud. AWS provides features such as TLS support, but the customer chooses to use them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which is AWS's responsibility under the shared responsibility model?","o":["Configuring network ACLs for a VPC","Writing IAM policies for developers","Encrypting data before uploading it to Amazon S3","Operating the Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS is responsible for the global infrastructure: Regions, Availability Zones, edge locations, and the hardware and software that run AWS services. Network ACLs, IAM policies, and client-side encryption are customer tasks."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company uses Amazon DynamoDB. Which tasks are AWS's responsibility? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Deciding which users can query the tables","Patching the servers that run the service","Deciding which data items to store","Physically securing the storage hardware","Choosing between an AWS owned key and a customer managed key for encryption"],"a":[1,3],"e":"For DynamoDB, AWS patches the servers and secures the physical hardware. Access control, the data itself, and the choice of encryption key are customer decisions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company runs a payment application on AWS, which is PCI DSS certified. What does this mean for the company?","o":["The application is automatically PCI DSS compliant","The company must still make its own application and configuration PCI DSS compliant, using AWS's attestation for the infrastructure","AWS performs the company's PCI DSS audit","The company no longer needs to encrypt cardholder data"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS's certification covers the infrastructure it manages; the customer is still responsible for compliance of its own workloads and configurations. Running on a compliant platform does not make an application automatically compliant."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A healthcare company must accept the AWS Business Associate Addendum (BAA) before storing protected health information. Where can it review and accept this agreement?","o":["AWS Artifact","AWS Config","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Security Hub"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Artifact provides both compliance reports and agreements such as the BAA, which can be accepted for an account or organization. Security Hub and Config assess your own resources; they do not hold AWS agreements."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A vendor-risk questionnaire asks for proof that AWS's own infrastructure has been independently audited. What should the company do?","o":["Run AWS Config rules and send the results","Send the AWS Trusted Advisor security checks","Export Amazon GuardDuty findings","Download the relevant AWS audit reports, such as SOC reports, from AWS Artifact"],"a":[3],"e":"Third-party audit reports about AWS itself come from AWS Artifact. AWS Config, GuardDuty, and Trusted Advisor only describe the company's own resources, not AWS's audited controls."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A compliance team wants to deploy a packaged set of AWS Config rules and remediation actions, aligned to operational best practices, across all accounts in its organization. What should it use?","o":["AWS Artifact reports","AWS Config conformance packs","Amazon Inspector","IAM Access Analyzer"],"a":[1],"e":"Conformance packs bundle Config rules and remediation actions that can be deployed as one unit across an organization. AWS Audit Manager, a look-alike for audit evidence, has not been open to new customers since April 30, 2026, and AWS recommends conformance packs instead; Artifact only provides AWS's own reports."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security engineer needs to see what a security group's inbound rules looked like last Tuesday, before someone changed them. Which service shows this?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS Config records configuration history, so you can view a resource's configuration at an earlier point in time. CloudWatch shows performance metrics and logs, not resource configuration history."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company requires every Amazon S3 bucket to have default encryption and wants noncompliant buckets fixed automatically. Which service meets this need?","o":["Amazon Macie","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config rules with automatic remediation","Amazon Detective"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Config rules evaluate resources against desired settings, and remediation actions (through Systems Manager Automation) can fix noncompliant resources. Macie looks for sensitive data inside objects; it does not enforce bucket settings."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An S3 bucket containing production data was deleted yesterday. Which service identifies the IAM identity that deleted it?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","Amazon Macie","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[0],"e":"CloudTrail logs API calls such as DeleteBucket, including the identity, time, and source IP. AWS Config can show that the bucket's configuration item was deleted, but CloudTrail is the record of who made the call."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"AWS Config shows that a security group rule changed overnight. Which service should the team use to find out WHO made the change?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch metrics","Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":[3],"e":"Config answers 'what changed and how is it configured', while CloudTrail answers 'who called which API and when'. Trusted Advisor only flags risky settings, such as unrestricted ports, without identifying who made them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company must prove that its AWS CloudTrail log files were not modified or deleted after delivery to Amazon S3. Which feature helps?","o":["S3 Transfer Acceleration","CloudTrail log file integrity validation","Amazon Macie sensitive data discovery","AWS Config conformance packs"],"a":[1],"e":"Log file integrity validation uses digest files with hashes and digital signatures to detect whether CloudTrail log files were changed or deleted. Macie looks for sensitive data, not log tampering."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company has not created any AWS CloudTrail trail. What can it still view in CloudTrail Event history?","o":["Nothing, because CloudTrail must be enabled first","All data events for the past 7 years","The past 90 days of management events","Only events made by the root user"],"a":[2],"e":"CloudTrail Event history shows the last 90 days of management events in a Region at no cost, without a trail. A trail (or CloudTrail Lake) is needed for longer retention and for data events such as S3 object-level activity."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team wants an alarm and email notification when the number of failed AWS Management Console sign-ins in CloudTrail logs exceeds a threshold. Which service provides the alarm?","o":["AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Artifact","Amazon Macie"],"a":[1],"e":"CloudTrail logs sent to CloudWatch Logs can use metric filters, and a CloudWatch alarm can notify through Amazon SNS when the metric crosses a threshold. AWS Config evaluates resource configurations, not counts of log events."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to collect operating system and application logs from its EC2 instances in one place to search them during a security investigation. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","AWS Artifact","AWS Config","Amazon CloudWatch Logs"],"a":[3],"e":"CloudWatch Logs (through the CloudWatch agent) collects, stores, and searches OS and application logs. CloudTrail records AWS API calls only, not logs written inside an instance."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An EC2 instance is fully patched but has started communicating with an IP address known for cryptocurrency mining. Which service is designed to detect this?","o":["Amazon Inspector","Amazon Macie","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config"],"a":[2],"e":"GuardDuty uses threat intelligence and machine learning on network and API activity to detect behavior such as cryptomining and command-and-control traffic. Inspector looks for software vulnerabilities, which a fully patched instance would not have."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"API calls are being made with an IAM user's credentials from an unusual location and a known malicious IP address. Which service generates a finding for this?","o":["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Artifact"],"a":[0],"e":"GuardDuty analyzes CloudTrail management events and flags anomalous or malicious API activity, such as possibly compromised credentials. Trusted Advisor checks configurations against best practices; it does not watch API activity for threats."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"What must a company do to start Amazon GuardDuty threat detection on its foundational data sources (CloudTrail management events, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs)?","o":["Install an agent on every EC2 instance","Create a CloudTrail trail and VPC Flow Logs first","Deploy a third-party intrusion detection appliance","Enable GuardDuty; it reads these sources directly with no agents to install"],"a":[3],"e":"GuardDuty pulls its foundational data sources directly from AWS, so you do not need to create trails or flow logs or install agents. Only optional features such as Runtime Monitoring use an agent."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company lets customers upload files to an Amazon S3 bucket and wants each new upload scanned for malware. Which service provides this?","o":["Amazon Macie","Amazon GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3","Amazon Inspector","AWS Shield Advanced"],"a":[1],"e":"GuardDuty Malware Protection for S3 scans newly uploaded objects for malware. Macie is the tempting distractor, but it discovers sensitive data such as PII; it does not detect malware."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Someone is reading large amounts of data from an Amazon S3 bucket through API calls from a known malicious IP address. Which service detects this suspicious activity?","o":["Amazon Macie","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config","AWS Artifact"],"a":[1],"e":"GuardDuty S3 Protection monitors S3 data-plane activity (such as GetObject calls) for suspicious access. Macie tells you WHAT sensitive data is in the bucket, not that a threat actor is reading it."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A development team pushes container images to Amazon ECR and wants each image checked for known software vulnerabilities (CVEs). Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Macie","Amazon Inspector","Amazon Detective"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon Inspector scans ECR container images, EC2 instances, and Lambda functions for known vulnerabilities. GuardDuty detects threats in activity, not vulnerable packages in images."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An AWS Lambda function uses an open source library that has a newly published critical vulnerability. Which service will report this automatically?","o":["Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config"],"a":[0],"e":"Inspector continuously scans Lambda function code dependencies and rescans when new CVEs are published. Trusted Advisor gives best-practice checks but does not scan software packages for CVEs."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to know which of its EC2 instances have open network paths reachable from the internet, together with their software vulnerabilities. Which service provides both kinds of findings?","o":["Amazon Macie","Amazon Cognito","AWS Artifact","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[3],"e":"Inspector produces package vulnerability findings and network reachability findings for EC2 instances. Macie works only on data in S3 and does not analyze network paths."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which service gives each software vulnerability finding a risk score that is adjusted for the environment, such as whether the affected instance is reachable from the internet?","o":["AWS Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config"],"a":[2],"e":"Inspector calculates an Inspector risk score by combining the CVE's base score with environmental context such as network reachability. GuardDuty assigns severity to threat findings, not to software vulnerabilities."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company must find out which of its Amazon S3 buckets contain credit card numbers and passport numbers. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Macie","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config"],"a":[1],"e":"Macie uses machine learning and pattern matching to discover sensitive data such as financial and personal identifiers in S3 objects. GuardDuty detects threats and does not inspect object contents for sensitive data."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A developer accidentally uploaded files containing AWS secret access keys and private keys to Amazon S3. Which service can discover these credentials inside the objects?","o":["Amazon Inspector","IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon Macie","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[2],"e":"Macie's managed data identifiers detect credentials such as AWS secret access keys and private keys, as well as PII, in S3 objects. IAM Access Analyzer finds resources shared outside your account, not secrets inside files."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants Amazon Macie to scan its data for sensitive information. Where must the data be stored?","o":["Amazon EBS volumes","Amazon RDS databases","Amazon DynamoDB tables","Amazon S3 buckets"],"a":[3],"e":"Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3. Data in EBS, RDS, or DynamoDB is not scanned by Macie directly."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants Amazon Macie to detect its internal employee ID format, which no built-in detector recognizes. What should it configure?","o":["A custom data identifier with a regular expression","An AWS Config custom rule","An Amazon GuardDuty threat list","An Amazon Inspector suppression rule"],"a":[0],"e":"Macie custom data identifiers use regular expressions (and optional keywords) to find organization-specific sensitive data. A GuardDuty threat list holds malicious IP addresses; it does not detect data patterns."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"In addition to finding sensitive data, which capability does Amazon Macie provide for Amazon S3?","o":["Scanning objects for malware","Encrypting objects automatically with AWS KMS","An inventory of buckets that shows whether each is public, shared, or unencrypted","Blocking DDoS attacks against bucket endpoints"],"a":[2],"e":"Macie keeps an inventory of S3 buckets and evaluates their security and access controls, such as public access and encryption. Malware scanning of S3 objects is a GuardDuty feature, not a Macie feature."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"After a GuardDuty finding, an analyst wants to visualize the IP addresses, IAM roles, and resources involved and see how their activity changed over time. Which service should the analyst use?","o":["Amazon Detective","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config","Amazon Macie"],"a":[0],"e":"Detective builds a behavior graph from log data so analysts can investigate the scope and root cause of findings over time. AWS Config shows resource configuration history, not the activity of IPs and identities involved in an incident."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which data sources does Amazon Detective automatically use to build its behavior graph? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Contents of objects in Amazon S3","AWS CloudTrail logs","Memory dumps from EC2 instances","Amazon VPC Flow Logs","Query results from Amazon RDS"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Detective ingests CloudTrail logs, VPC Flow Logs, and GuardDuty findings (plus optional sources such as EKS audit logs). It does not read object contents, instance memory, or database query results."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team receives hundreds of GuardDuty findings each week. For one high-severity finding, it needs to determine how far an attacker got and what else was affected. Which service is designed for this?","o":["AWS Security Hub","Amazon Detective","AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[1],"e":"Detective is built for investigating the scope and root cause of security findings. Security Hub aggregates findings and checks posture, but it is not a deep investigation tool."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company with 40 AWS accounts wants one place that collects findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, and Macie across all accounts and helps prioritize them. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Detective","AWS Config","AWS Security Hub","AWS Artifact"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS Security Hub centralizes and prioritizes security findings across services and accounts. Detective investigates individual findings rather than serving as the central place where all findings are collected."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants automated checks of its AWS accounts against the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark and the AWS Foundational Security Best Practices standard. Which service provides these checks?","o":["AWS Security Hub","Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","AWS Artifact"],"a":[0],"e":"Security Hub runs automated controls against standards such as CIS and AWS Foundational Security Best Practices. AWS Artifact provides AWS's own compliance reports, not checks of your accounts."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which service must be enabled for AWS Security Hub to run most of its automated security controls?","o":["Amazon Detective","AWS Shield Advanced","Amazon Macie","AWS Config"],"a":[3],"e":"Security Hub evaluates most controls using AWS Config rules, so AWS Config must record the relevant resources. Detective is an investigation service and is not required for these checks."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which service checks an account for security issues such as security groups that allow unrestricted access to specific ports and whether MFA is enabled on the root user, alongside cost and performance recommendations?","o":["Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor","Amazon Macie","AWS Artifact"],"a":[1],"e":"Trusted Advisor checks accounts against best practices across categories including security, cost optimization, and performance. Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and does not give cost recommendations."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which pairing of service and purpose is correct?","o":["Amazon Inspector: detecting compromised credentials from CloudTrail activity","Amazon Detective: scanning container images for CVEs","Amazon Macie: discovering sensitive data such as PII in Amazon S3","Amazon GuardDuty: downloading AWS compliance reports"],"a":[2],"e":"Macie discovers sensitive data in S3. Compromised-credential detection is GuardDuty, image CVE scanning is Inspector, and compliance reports come from AWS Artifact."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which pairing of service and purpose is accurate?","o":["AWS Config: recording which IAM user made an API call","Amazon GuardDuty: detecting threats such as unusual API calls and malicious network traffic","AWS Artifact: aggregating findings from Inspector","Amazon Inspector: discovering PII in S3"],"a":[1],"e":"GuardDuty is the threat detection service. API call records are CloudTrail, aggregating findings is Security Hub, and PII discovery is Macie."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company must (1) find unpatched software vulnerabilities on its EC2 instances and (2) find personal data stored in its S3 buckets. Which services should it use? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","Amazon Detective","AWS Artifact","Amazon Macie"],"a":[1,4],"e":"Inspector finds software vulnerabilities, and Macie finds sensitive data in S3. GuardDuty detects active threats, and Detective investigates findings; neither is a vulnerability scanner or a PII scanner."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An auditor asks for (1) a record of who called which AWS APIs and (2) the configuration history of each resource. Which services provide this? (Choose TWO.)","o":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config","Amazon Macie","AWS Shield"],"a":[0,2],"e":"CloudTrail records API activity (who, what, when), and AWS Config records resource configuration history. Inspector and Macie scan for vulnerabilities and sensitive data, not activity or configuration history."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statement correctly describes how Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Detective, and AWS Security Hub relate?","o":["GuardDuty detects threats, Detective investigates them, and Security Hub centralizes findings from many services","Detective detects threats, GuardDuty investigates them, and Security Hub scans for CVEs","Security Hub detects threats, GuardDuty stores compliance reports, and Detective scans S3","All three are different names for the same service"],"a":[0],"e":"GuardDuty generates threat findings, Detective helps investigate their root cause, and Security Hub brings findings together for prioritization. None of these three scans for CVEs; that is Inspector."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which service analyzes the CONTENT of data, rather than activity or configuration, to identify sensitive information?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config","Amazon Macie"],"a":[3],"e":"Macie inspects the contents of S3 objects to classify sensitive data. CloudTrail and GuardDuty work with activity, and AWS Config works with resource configuration."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A team wants to check for known CVEs in the operating system packages installed on its EC2 instances. Why is AWS Trusted Advisor NOT the right tool?","o":["Trusted Advisor works only for Amazon S3","Trusted Advisor only reports on costs","Trusted Advisor only works for the root user","Trusted Advisor checks account configurations against best practices but does not scan installed software for vulnerabilities; Amazon Inspector does"],"a":[3],"e":"Inspector is the AWS vulnerability scanner for EC2, ECR, and Lambda. Trusted Advisor gives account-level best-practice checks (security, cost, performance, and more) but no package-level CVE scanning."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants a rule that every EBS volume must be encrypted, with a compliance timeline for each volume showing when it was compliant or noncompliant. Which service provides this?","o":["AWS Config","Amazon GuardDuty","Amazon Detective","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS Config rules evaluate each resource and keep a compliance timeline for it. CloudTrail records the API calls that created the volumes, but it does not evaluate them against rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which is an example of a PREVENTIVE control, rather than a detective control?","o":["An Amazon GuardDuty finding about unusual API calls","An AWS Config rule that flags unencrypted volumes","A service control policy (SCP) that denies disabling AWS CloudTrail","An Amazon Detective investigation"],"a":[2],"e":"An SCP blocks the action before it happens, so it is preventive. GuardDuty, Config rules, and Detective identify or investigate issues after they occur, so they are detective controls."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A developer uploads a new object to Amazon S3 without specifying any encryption settings. What happens?","o":["The object is stored unencrypted","The upload fails","The object is encrypted only if the bucket is public","The object is automatically encrypted at rest with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3)"],"a":[3],"e":"Since January 2023, S3 automatically encrypts all new objects with SSE-S3 by default. Customers can choose SSE-KMS or other options for more control over keys."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants all new Amazon EBS volumes in a Region to be encrypted automatically, even if a developer forgets to select encryption. What should it do?","o":["Enable EBS encryption by default for the account in that Region","Enable Amazon Macie","Attach a security group to each volume","Turn on AWS Shield Standard"],"a":[0],"e":"EBS encryption by default is an account setting per Region that encrypts every new volume and snapshot copy. Macie discovers sensitive data in S3 and does not encrypt EBS volumes."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to control the key policy for its encryption keys, decide who can use them, and enable or disable them as needed. Which type of key should it use?","o":["AWS managed keys","AWS owned keys","Customer managed keys in AWS KMS","Amazon EC2 key pairs"],"a":[2],"e":"Customer managed keys give full control over key policies, rotation, and enabling or disabling. AWS managed keys are created for a service on your behalf, and you cannot change their key policies."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team must audit every time an AWS KMS key is used to decrypt data. Where are these events recorded?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Macie","AWS Artifact","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[0],"e":"KMS is integrated with CloudTrail, which logs each API request such as Decrypt and Encrypt with the caller and time. Macie inspects S3 data, not key usage."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company encrypts data on its own servers before uploading it to Amazon S3 and keeps the keys on premises. What is this called?","o":["Server-side encryption with S3 managed keys","Encryption in transit","Client-side encryption","Server-side encryption with KMS keys"],"a":[2],"e":"Client-side encryption means data is encrypted before it reaches AWS, so the customer manages the keys and the process. Server-side encryption happens after AWS receives the data."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which are examples of protecting data IN TRANSIT? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Enabling SSE-KMS on an S3 bucket","Connecting to Amazon S3 over HTTPS (TLS)","Encrypting an Amazon RDS DB instance's storage","Encrypting an EBS snapshot","Sending traffic through an AWS Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec) tunnel"],"a":[1,4],"e":"TLS and IPsec VPN tunnels protect data while it moves across a network. SSE-KMS, RDS storage encryption, and EBS snapshot encryption protect data at rest."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which actions encrypt data AT REST? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Using an HTTPS listener on an Application Load Balancer","Enabling encryption when creating an Amazon RDS DB instance","Requesting a public certificate from AWS Certificate Manager","Configuring an S3 bucket's default encryption to use SSE-KMS","Creating an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection"],"a":[1,3],"e":"RDS storage encryption and S3 SSE-KMS protect stored data. HTTPS listeners, ACM certificates, and VPN connections protect data in transit."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company is required by law to keep customer data inside Germany. How can it meet this requirement on AWS?","o":["Store data in an AWS Region in Germany; AWS does not move customer content out of the chosen Region unless the customer does so","Use any Region, because AWS automatically keeps data in the customer's home country","Use Amazon CloudFront to keep the data in Germany","Enable AWS Shield Advanced for data residency"],"a":[0],"e":"Customers choose the Region where their content is stored, and AWS does not move it to another Region without the customer's action. CloudFront caches content at edge locations worldwide, so it does not help keep data in one country."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company must make sure no account in its AWS organization can create resources outside approved European Regions. What is the most effective control?","o":["An IAM password policy","A service control policy (SCP) that denies actions in other Regions","AWS Trusted Advisor checks","An Amazon Macie job"],"a":[1],"e":"An SCP can deny requests to Regions that are not approved (for example, with the aws:RequestedRegion condition) for every account in the organization. Trusted Advisor can only report issues; it cannot prevent actions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to know whether a specific AWS service can be used for workloads under a compliance program such as HIPAA or FedRAMP. Where should it look?","o":["The AWS Services in Scope by Compliance Program page","The Amazon GuardDuty console","AWS Trusted Advisor cost checks","The AWS Pricing Calculator"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS publishes which services are in scope for each compliance program on its compliance website. GuardDuty and Trusted Advisor assess your account and do not list program scope."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A user signs in with a password and then enters a code from an authenticator app on their phone. What is this an example of?","o":["Single sign-on","Multi-factor authentication (MFA)","Federation","Access key authentication"],"a":[1],"e":"MFA combines something you know (a password) with something you have (a device that generates codes or a security key). Single sign-on lets one sign-in give access to many applications, which is a different concept."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which MFA types does AWS IAM support for console sign-in? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Codes sent by SMS text message","Virtual authenticator apps","Codes sent by email","FIDO2 passkeys and security keys","Security questions"],"a":[1,3],"e":"IAM supports passkeys and FIDO security keys, virtual authenticator apps, and hardware TOTP tokens. SMS-based MFA is no longer supported for IAM, and email codes and security questions are not MFA devices."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to require MFA before anyone can permanently delete object versions in a versioned S3 bucket. Who must enable MFA Delete on the bucket?","o":["Any IAM administrator","The AWS Trust & Safety team","An IAM role with S3 full access","The AWS account root user"],"a":[3],"e":"Only the bucket owner's root user can enable MFA Delete on an S3 bucket. IAM administrators cannot turn it on, even with full S3 permissions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"The only IAM administrator in an account accidentally removed their own permissions, and no other IAM user can manage IAM. How can access be restored?","o":["Open a ticket with AWS Trust & Safety","Sign in as the AWS account root user and restore the IAM permissions","Use IAM Access Analyzer to repair the policy","Wait for AWS Config to roll back the change"],"a":[1],"e":"Restoring IAM permissions when all administrators are locked out is a root user task. Access Analyzer can analyze policies but cannot sign in or change permissions on your behalf."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to sell its unused Standard Reserved Instances on the Reserved Instance Marketplace. Which identity must register as a seller?","o":["An IAM user with billing permissions","An IAM role in the management account","The AWS account root user","Any user in IAM Identity Center"],"a":[2],"e":"Registering as a seller in the Reserved Instance Marketplace is one of the tasks only the root user can do. IAM users and roles cannot complete seller registration."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security review finds that the AWS account root user has an active access key. What is the best practice?","o":["Rotate the key every 90 days","Store the key in AWS Secrets Manager","Share the key only with senior administrators","Delete the root user access key"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS recommends that the root user have no access keys at all, because they grant unrestricted access to the account. Storing or rotating the key still leaves an unrestricted long-term credential in place."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants all IAM user passwords to be at least 14 characters long and to include symbols. What should it configure?","o":["An IAM account password policy","A service control policy","An AWS Config conformance pack","An S3 bucket policy"],"a":[0],"e":"The IAM account password policy sets length, complexity, expiration, and reuse rules for IAM user passwords. It does not apply to the root user. SCPs limit permissions; they do not set password rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An auditor wants a downloadable list of all IAM users in the account showing whether each has a password, active access keys, and MFA enabled. What should the administrator provide?","o":["IAM Access Analyzer findings","The IAM credential report","AWS Artifact reports","Amazon GuardDuty findings"],"a":[1],"e":"The IAM credential report lists every IAM user and the status of their passwords, access keys, and MFA devices. Access Analyzer finds resources shared externally and unused access, not a per-user credential inventory."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An administrator wants to remove permissions an IAM role no longer uses. Which feature shows which AWS services the role accessed and when it last accessed them?","o":["The IAM credential report","AWS Shield","AWS Artifact","IAM last accessed information (access advisor)"],"a":[3],"e":"Last accessed information (access advisor) shows when each service was last used, which helps remove unused permissions for least privilege. The credential report covers user credentials such as passwords and keys, not service usage by roles."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to find any S3 buckets, KMS keys, or IAM roles in its account that grant access to principals outside its AWS organization. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Macie","IAM Access Analyzer","Amazon Inspector","AWS Trusted Advisor"],"a":[1],"e":"IAM Access Analyzer uses automated reasoning on resource policies to find resources shared with external principals. Macie can report on public or shared S3 buckets, but it does not analyze KMS keys or IAM role trust policies."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which capabilities does IAM Access Analyzer provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Scanning EC2 instances for software vulnerabilities","Identifying resources shared with external entities","Rotating database passwords","Generating least-privilege policies based on CloudTrail activity","Discovering PII in S3 objects"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Access Analyzer finds external and unused access, validates policies, and can generate policies from CloudTrail activity. Vulnerability scanning is Inspector, password rotation is Secrets Manager, and PII discovery is Macie."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"External auditors who work in their own AWS account need read-only access to resources in a company's account. What is the most secure approach?","o":["Create IAM users with passwords for the auditors in the company's account","Share the root user credentials","Create an IAM role with read-only permissions that the auditors' account is trusted to assume","Make the resources public for the audit period"],"a":[2],"e":"A cross-account IAM role gives temporary credentials and avoids creating and managing long-term users in your account. Creating IAM users for outsiders works but adds long-term credentials that must be managed and removed later."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"When an IAM role is assumed, which service issues the temporary security credentials?","o":["AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS)","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS KMS"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS STS issues short-term credentials (access key, secret key, and session token) that expire automatically. Secrets Manager stores secrets you create; it does not issue role credentials."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"What do an access key ID and secret access key allow an IAM user to do?","o":["Sign in to the AWS Management Console","Make programmatic requests through the AWS CLI, SDKs, or APIs","Encrypt EBS volumes","Connect to an EC2 instance through SSH"],"a":[1],"e":"Access keys are long-term credentials for programmatic access. Console sign-in uses a password (and MFA), and SSH to EC2 uses a separate key pair."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A developer finds that an IAM user's access key was pushed to a public code repository. What should be done FIRST?","o":["Delete the repository and keep using the key","Enable AWS Shield Advanced","Change the IAM user's console password","Deactivate the exposed access key and create a new one if still needed"],"a":[3],"e":"An exposed key must be deactivated or deleted immediately, then CloudTrail activity for that key should be reviewed. Changing the console password does not stop anyone from using the leaked access key."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An IAM user has one policy that allows s3:DeleteObject and another that explicitly denies s3:DeleteObject. What happens when the user tries to delete an object?","o":["The request is denied, because an explicit deny always overrides an allow","The request is allowed, because allow is evaluated first","The most recently attached policy wins","The request is allowed only with MFA"],"a":[0],"e":"In IAM policy evaluation, an explicit deny overrides any allow. By default, all requests are implicitly denied unless something allows them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A new IAM user has been created with no policies attached and is not in any group. What can the user do?","o":["Everything, until a policy restricts them","Read-only actions on all services","Nothing, because all requests are implicitly denied by default","Only actions in the AWS Free Tier"],"a":[2],"e":"IAM denies everything by default; permissions must be granted explicitly. This default deny supports the principle of least privilege."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An Amazon S3 bucket policy that grants another AWS account access to a bucket is an example of which policy type?","o":["Identity-based policy","Permissions boundary","Service control policy","Resource-based policy"],"a":[3],"e":"A bucket policy is attached to the resource and names the principals who can access it, so it is resource-based. Identity-based policies are attached to users, groups, or roles."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which type of IAM policy is created and maintained by AWS and updated by AWS when new services or API actions are introduced?","o":["Inline policies","Customer managed policies","AWS managed policies","Service control policies"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS managed policies are maintained by AWS, which makes them convenient but sometimes broader than needed. Customer managed policies are written and maintained by you and can be scoped more tightly for least privilege."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements about IAM groups are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["A user can belong to more than one group","A group can be named as the principal in an S3 bucket policy","Groups cannot contain other groups","Groups have their own access keys","The root user can be added to a group"],"a":[0,2],"e":"An IAM user can be in several groups, and groups cannot be nested. Groups are not principals, so they cannot be named in resource-based policies, and they have no credentials of their own."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants team leads to create IAM roles for their applications, but no role they create may ever have more permissions than a set limit. Which IAM feature enforces this?","o":["Permissions boundaries","IAM groups","The credential report","Access keys"],"a":[0],"e":"A permissions boundary sets the maximum permissions an IAM user or role can have, which lets you safely delegate role creation. SCPs set limits for whole accounts, not for individual roles created by a team lead."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Several engineers share one IAM user's credentials. Why is this a problem?","o":["IAM users cannot sign in more than once a day","Actions in CloudTrail cannot be traced to an individual person","Shared users are billed at a higher rate","Shared users cannot use MFA"],"a":[1],"e":"Each person should have their own identity so that activity logged in CloudTrail is traceable to an individual and access can be revoked per person. Sharing credentials breaks this accountability."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Employees already sign in with the company's corporate identity provider (such as Microsoft Entra ID or Okta). The company wants them to use the same credentials to access multiple AWS accounts. Which service should it use?","o":["Amazon Cognito","AWS IAM Identity Center","IAM users in every account","AWS Secrets Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"IAM Identity Center connects to external identity providers and gives the workforce single sign-on to AWS accounts and applications. Cognito is for customer-facing app users, not employees accessing AWS accounts."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"In AWS IAM Identity Center, what defines the level of access that a user or group receives in an assigned AWS account?","o":["A permission set","A security group","A network ACL","A Cognito user pool"],"a":[0],"e":"Permission sets are collections of policies that Identity Center provisions as roles in the assigned accounts. Cognito user pools manage app user directories, not workforce access to AWS accounts."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A mobile app needs customer sign-up and sign-in, including sign-in with social identity providers such as Google and Apple. Which service should the developers use?","o":["AWS IAM Identity Center","IAM users for each customer","Amazon Cognito","AWS Organizations"],"a":[2],"e":"Amazon Cognito provides sign-up, sign-in, and social and enterprise federation for application end users. IAM Identity Center is for workforce users who need access to AWS accounts, not millions of app customers."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which capabilities does Amazon Cognito provide? (Choose TWO.)","o":["A user directory with sign-up and sign-in for web and mobile app users","Single sign-on for employees to multiple AWS accounts","Temporary AWS credentials so app users can access AWS resources such as S3 directly","Permission sets for AWS accounts","Service control policies for an organization"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Cognito user pools handle app user sign-up and sign-in, and identity pools exchange identities for temporary AWS credentials. Workforce SSO and permission sets belong to IAM Identity Center, and SCPs belong to AWS Organizations."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An SCP attached to a member account allows s3:*. An IAM user in that account has no IAM policies attached. Can the user access Amazon S3?","o":["Yes, because the SCP grants S3 access","Yes, but only read access","No, because the account's root user must approve","No, because SCPs never grant permissions; an identity-based or resource-based policy must also allow the action"],"a":[3],"e":"SCPs only set the maximum available permissions; they do not grant anything. The user also needs an identity-based or resource-based policy that allows the action."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements about service control policies (SCPs) in AWS Organizations are correct? (Choose TWO.)","o":["SCPs grant permissions to IAM users","SCPs can restrict the root user of a member account","SCPs restrict the users and roles in the management account","SCPs can be attached to the organization root, OUs, or individual accounts","SCPs replace the need for IAM policies"],"a":[1,3],"e":"SCPs apply to all principals in member accounts, including the root user, and can be attached at the root, OU, or account level. They do not grant permissions and do not affect the management account."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An application needs to store non-secret configuration values such as feature flags and AMI IDs in a hierarchy, with no additional charge for standard parameters. Which service fits best?","o":["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS KMS","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store"],"a":[3],"e":"Parameter Store holds configuration data and secrets, with standard parameters at no additional charge. Secrets Manager charges per secret and is designed for secrets that need lifecycle features such as automatic rotation."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An application stores a database password in its source code. What is the recommended way to fix this?","o":["Encode the password in Base64 in the code","Store the password in an Amazon S3 object","Store the password in AWS Secrets Manager and retrieve it at runtime","Put the password in the EC2 instance tags"],"a":[2],"e":"Secrets Manager stores secrets encrypted with KMS, controls access with IAM, logs access in CloudTrail, and can rotate them automatically. Base64 is encoding, not encryption, so anyone with the code can read the password."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to manage access to its EC2 instances without opening inbound SSH port 22 or running bastion hosts, and wants sessions to be logged. Which feature should it use?","o":["AWS Systems Manager Session Manager","Amazon Inspector","AWS Shield Standard","Amazon Macie"],"a":[0],"e":"Session Manager gives shell access through the Systems Manager agent, controlled by IAM, with no inbound ports needed, and it can log sessions. Inspector finds vulnerabilities but does not provide instance access."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"An administrator creates a new security group and adds no rules. What traffic does it allow?","o":["All inbound and all outbound traffic","No inbound traffic, and all outbound traffic","All inbound traffic, and no outbound traffic","Inbound traffic from the same VPC only"],"a":[1],"e":"A new security group has no inbound rules, so all inbound traffic is denied, and it has a default rule that allows all outbound traffic. You add allow rules for the inbound traffic you need."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company must block all traffic from one malicious IP address to every instance in a subnet. Which control can do this?","o":["A deny rule in a security group","A network ACL deny rule for that IP address","An IAM policy","AWS Shield Standard"],"a":[1],"e":"Network ACLs support explicit deny rules at the subnet level. Security groups only support allow rules, so you cannot add a deny rule for a single IP address."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"In what order are network ACL rules evaluated?","o":["All rules are evaluated, and the most permissive wins","Deny rules first, then allow rules","In order from the lowest rule number, and the first matching rule is applied","In the order they were created"],"a":[2],"e":"Network ACL rules are processed in ascending rule number order, and evaluation stops at the first match. Security groups work differently: all their rules are evaluated together."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A network ACL allows inbound HTTP on port 80, but users never receive responses from the web server. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The security group is stateless","The IAM role is missing permissions","AWS Shield is blocking the responses","Outbound rules for return traffic on ephemeral ports are missing, because network ACLs are stateless"],"a":[3],"e":"Network ACLs are stateless, so return traffic must be explicitly allowed (usually on ephemeral ports). Security groups are stateful and allow return traffic automatically."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A database should accept connections only from instances in the application tier. What is the best way to configure this?","o":["Allow the database port in the database security group from the application tier's security group","Allow the database port from 0.0.0.0/0","Add a deny rule for every other IP address in the security group","Place the database in a public subnet"],"a":[0],"e":"Security groups can reference other security groups as a source, so only members of the application tier can connect, even as their IP addresses change. Security groups do not support deny rules."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A website is being hit by an HTTP flood where single IP addresses send thousands of requests per minute. Which service can automatically block IP addresses that exceed a request rate?","o":["AWS WAF with a rate-based rule","Network ACLs","Amazon Inspector","AWS Config"],"a":[0],"e":"AWS WAF rate-based rules count requests per IP address and block IPs that exceed the limit. Network ACLs filter static IP ranges and cannot track request rates."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to block HTTP requests to its web application that come from specific countries. Which service can do this at layer 7?","o":["AWS Shield Standard","Security groups","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS WAF with a geographic match rule"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS WAF can allow or block web requests based on country of origin with geo match rules. Security groups filter by IP, port, and protocol only, without any country awareness."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"On which resources can AWS WAF be deployed? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Network Load Balancer","Amazon CloudFront distributions","Amazon EBS volumes","Application Load Balancer","Amazon EC2 instances directly"],"a":[1,3],"e":"AWS WAF protects resources such as CloudFront, Application Load Balancers, API Gateway REST APIs, and AWS AppSync. It does not attach to Network Load Balancers, EBS volumes, or individual EC2 instances."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statement about AWS Shield Standard is correct?","o":["It must be purchased separately for each resource","It includes 24/7 access to the Shield Response Team","It is automatically enabled for all AWS customers at no additional charge","It protects only against application-layer (layer 7) attacks"],"a":[2],"e":"Shield Standard is on by default at no extra cost and protects against common network and transport layer (layer 3 and 4) DDoS attacks such as SYN floods. Shield Response Team access is part of Shield Advanced."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company is planning for large DDoS attacks against its public applications. Which benefits does it get only with AWS Shield Advanced? (Choose TWO.)","o":["24/7 access to the AWS Shield Response Team (SRT)","Protection against common layer 3 and layer 4 attacks","Cost protection for scaling charges caused by DDoS attacks","Automatic enablement at no additional cost","Encryption of data at rest"],"a":[0,2],"e":"Shield Advanced adds SRT access, cost protection, and advanced detection and visibility. Basic layer 3 and 4 protection is already included free in Shield Standard."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company with many AWS accounts wants to centrally apply AWS WAF rules and security group policies to all accounts, including new accounts as they are created. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Network Firewall","AWS Shield Standard","Amazon Inspector"],"a":[0],"e":"Firewall Manager centrally manages WAF rules, Shield Advanced protections, security group policies, and Network Firewall across an organization. Network Firewall is the firewall itself for VPC traffic, not the central policy manager."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"What is a prerequisite for using AWS Firewall Manager?","o":["The accounts must be members of AWS Organizations","Every account must purchase Shield Advanced","The accounts must use AWS CloudHSM","Each account needs Amazon Inspector enabled"],"a":[0],"e":"Firewall Manager works across accounts in AWS Organizations (and requires AWS Config to be enabled). Shield Advanced is needed only if you want to manage Shield Advanced protections."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company's central networking account manages VPC subnets and a transit gateway. It wants other accounts in its AWS organization to use these resources instead of creating their own. Which service lets it share them?","o":["AWS Firewall Manager","AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","VPC peering","AWS Artifact"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS RAM securely shares resources such as subnets, transit gateways, and Route 53 Resolver rules with other accounts or the whole organization, so they do not need duplicates. Firewall Manager centrally applies security rules such as AWS WAF and security group policies but does not share resources, and VPC peering connects two VPCs without sharing subnets."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which service can automatically rotate the key material of a symmetric encryption key used by Amazon S3 and Amazon EBS?","o":["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS KMS","IAM Access Analyzer"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS KMS supports automatic rotation of the key material for customer managed symmetric keys, and AWS managed keys are rotated automatically. Secrets Manager rotates secrets such as database passwords, not KMS keys."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to encrypt data in S3, EBS, and RDS with the least operational overhead, without managing hardware. Which service should it use?","o":["AWS CloudHSM","AWS KMS","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS Secrets Manager"],"a":[1],"e":"AWS KMS is fully managed and integrated with S3, EBS, RDS, and many other services. CloudHSM gives dedicated HSMs under your exclusive control, but you manage the users, keys, and cluster, which adds overhead."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A website on Amazon CloudFront suffered an outage when its SSL/TLS certificate expired. Which service would have prevented this by renewing the certificate automatically?","o":["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS CloudHSM"],"a":[0],"e":"ACM-issued public certificates are renewed automatically when they are in use with integrated services such as CloudFront and ELB. KMS manages encryption keys, not TLS certificates."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to deploy a next-generation firewall product from a well-known security vendor that it already uses on premises. Where can it find and deploy this product on AWS?","o":["AWS Artifact","AWS Security Hub","AWS Trusted Advisor","AWS Marketplace"],"a":[3],"e":"AWS Marketplace offers third-party software, including security products such as firewalls, IDS/IPS, and antivirus, ready to deploy on AWS. AWS Artifact provides compliance documents, not software."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company sees that an IP address belonging to AWS is sending spam and port scanning its systems. Whom should it contact?","o":["AWS Support through its own Basic plan","The AWS Trust & Safety team, through the abuse reporting form","AWS Professional Services","The AWS Shield Response Team"],"a":[1],"e":"The AWS Trust & Safety team handles reports of AWS resources used for abuse, such as spam, port scanning, DDoS, or phishing. The Shield Response Team helps Shield Advanced customers during attacks on their own resources, not abuse reports."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Where should a security engineer look for AWS security articles, best practices, and announcements of new security features?","o":["The AWS Security Blog","AWS Artifact","The AWS Pricing Calculator","AWS Cost Explorer"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Security Blog publishes security best practices, how-to guides, and feature announcements. AWS Artifact only provides compliance reports and agreements."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A developer wants AWS-written answers to common questions, such as how to troubleshoot an access denied error on an S3 bucket. Which resource is most suitable?","o":["AWS Knowledge Center","AWS Artifact","AWS Organizations","AWS Marketplace"],"a":[0],"e":"The AWS Knowledge Center (part of AWS re:Post) has articles answering frequent customer questions and troubleshooting steps. AWS Marketplace sells third-party software and does not offer troubleshooting articles."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to run a penetration test against its own Amazon EC2 instances. What is AWS's policy?","o":["It is never allowed","It requires a signed contract with AWS Professional Services","Customers can test approved services such as EC2 without prior approval, but prohibited activities such as DoS attacks are not allowed","It is allowed only with AWS Shield Advanced"],"a":[2],"e":"AWS allows security assessments of listed services, such as EC2, RDS, and Lambda, without prior approval, as long as they follow the policy. Activities such as DoS testing and flooding are prohibited or follow a separate stress-test policy."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A security team wants to record information about accepted and rejected IP traffic going to and from the network interfaces in a VPC. Which feature should it enable?","o":["AWS CloudTrail","VPC Flow Logs","AWS Config","Amazon Macie"],"a":[1],"e":"VPC Flow Logs capture IP traffic metadata (source, destination, ports, accept or reject) for network interfaces. CloudTrail records AWS API calls, not network packets or flows."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants to make sure that no S3 bucket in its account can ever be made public, even if a bucket policy tries to allow public access. What should it enable?","o":["S3 Block Public Access at the account level","Amazon Macie","S3 Versioning","S3 Transfer Acceleration"],"a":[0],"e":"Account-level S3 Block Public Access overrides bucket policies and ACLs that would grant public access. Macie can report public buckets, but it does not prevent them."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company wants AWS API activity to be logged for every account in its organization, including accounts created in the future. What should it set up?","o":["A CloudTrail trail in each account created manually","AWS Trusted Advisor checks","Amazon CloudWatch dashboards","An AWS CloudTrail organization trail created from the management account"],"a":[3],"e":"An organization trail logs events for all member accounts, including new ones, and member accounts cannot change it. Creating trails manually in each account is error-prone and misses new accounts."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company protects its web application with AWS WAF on CloudFront, security groups on its instances, and network ACLs on its subnets. Which security principle does this layered approach demonstrate?","o":["Least privilege","Defense in depth","Separation of duties","Shared responsibility"],"a":[1],"e":"Defense in depth uses multiple layers of security controls so that if one fails, others still protect the workload. Least privilege is about limiting permissions, not layering controls."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"A company runs its API on AWS Lambda. Which tasks are the customer's responsibility? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Patching the operating system of the Lambda execution environment","Writing secure function code and keeping its dependencies up to date","Maintaining the physical servers that run the functions","Granting the function's execution role only the permissions it needs","Patching the managed language runtime"],"a":[1,3],"e":"With Lambda, the customer owns its code, dependencies, and the IAM permissions of the execution role. AWS patches the execution environment OS, the managed runtimes, and the physical servers."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which services analyze activity such as CloudTrail logs and VPC Flow Logs to detect or investigate threats? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Amazon Macie","Amazon Inspector","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Artifact","Amazon Detective"],"a":[2,4],"e":"GuardDuty detects threats from activity logs, and Detective uses the same kinds of logs to investigate findings. Macie inspects S3 data content and Inspector scans software for vulnerabilities, so neither analyzes activity logs for threats."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which tasks can be performed ONLY by the AWS account root user? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Create an IAM role","Change the account's root user email address","Enable Amazon GuardDuty","Close the AWS account","Create a CloudWatch billing alarm"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Changing the root email address and closing the account are root-only tasks. Creating roles, enabling GuardDuty, and creating alarms can be done by any IAM principal with the right permissions."},
  {"d":2,"t":"security","q":"Which statements correctly compare AWS Secrets Manager and AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store? (Choose TWO.)","o":["Parameter Store cannot store encrypted values","Secrets Manager has built-in automatic rotation for secrets such as database credentials","Secrets Manager secrets are free of charge","Parameter Store standard parameters are available at no additional charge","Parameter Store can store only TLS certificates"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Secrets Manager adds managed automatic rotation and charges per secret, while Parameter Store standard parameters cost nothing extra. Parameter Store can also store encrypted values as SecureString parameters using KMS."},
];
